<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-10.75%" pitch="-2.60%">
            Now David is the youngest son of Jesse the Bethlehemite, and Jesse has eight sons.<break time="500ms"/>
      The three eldest follow Saul to the battle, but David goes back and forth from Saul to feed his father's sheep at Bethlehem.<break time="1000ms"/>
      He is a shepherd boy, tending the flock in the hills, far from the camp and the noise of armies.<break time="500ms"/>
      Jesse says to David, take now for your brothers an ephah of this parched grain and these ten loaves, and carry ten cheeses to the commander of their thousand.<break time="500ms"/>
      See how your brothers fare, and bring some token from them.<break time="1000ms"/>
      So David rises early in the morning and leaves the sheep with a keeper and goes as Jesse has commanded him.<break time="500ms"/>
      He comes to the camp as the army is going out to the battle line, shouting the war cry.<break time="500ms"/>
      And as he speaks with his brothers, the champion, Goliath of Gath, comes out from the Philistine ranks and speaks his same words.<break time="1000ms"/>
      David hears them.<break time="500ms"/>
      All the men of Israel, when they see the man, flee from him and are much afraid.<break time="500ms"/>
      But David does not flee.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The shepherd boy stands still where the soldiers run.<break time="1000ms"/>
      He has come with bread and cheese, not with a sword, and yet he does not turn away.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Something stirs in David that the army has forgotten.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>